systems.core:
To find an item in an unsorted collection, inspecting every element one by one takes time proportional to the collection size. If you have a million items, you might perform a million comparisons. A **binary search** drastically speeds this up by comparing your target value against the middle element of an ordered array. If your target is smaller, the upper half of the search space is discarded; if larger, the lower half is eliminated. Repeating this process cuts the remaining elements in half at every step, finding any item in logarithmic time.

Here is a minimal implementation in Python:

def binary_search(arr, target):
    low, high = 0, len(arr) - 1
    while low <= high:
        mid = (low + high) // 2
        if arr[mid] == target:
            return mid
        elif arr[mid] < target:
            low = mid + 1
        else:
            high = mid - 1
    return -1

A common bug beginners introduce is an off-by-one error in index bounds, such as writing low < high instead of low <= high, which skips checking the final remaining element. In low-level languages like C, calculating mid via (low + high) / 2 can also cause an **integer overflow** when working with huge arrays. The primary trade-off of this structure is that it strictly requires **sorted data**. Because sorting takes upfront compute, binary search excels when you search a stable dataset frequently.
